On Thu, 2016-08-11 at 11:21 +0200, Haakon Bakka wrote:
> Additionally, you can do it in the rgeneric framework.
> (see inla.doc('rgeneric'))
>
> Here, you would fix the likelihood precision to a small value, and
> then define your own random effect N(0, sigma) to add on to the
> linear predictor.
>
> What you would need to construct is the precision (inverse
> covariance) matrix for this new random effect as a function of two
> hyperparameters Gamma_0 and Gamma_1. I think this matrix would just
> be a diagonal, and therefore very easy to implement.
>
> @Håvard: Maybe this would be a way to implement it for everyone in
> the future? (As a random effect with a fixed-noise likelihood.)

this is correct but only valid for a Gaussian likelihood. Extending a
Gamma-likelihood, for example, cannot be done in this way.

--
Håvard Rue
